prompt:
You are a financial analyst revising a forecast based on a peer critique.

Original forecast: {
  "strategy": "alt_strategy",
  "base_year": 2022,
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "forecast": [
    {
      "year": 2023,
      "revenue_cr": 143534,
      "net_income_cr": 25127,
      "eps": 61.5
    },
    {
      "year": 2024,
      "revenue_cr": 163029,
      "net_income_cr": 27640,
      "eps": 68.9
    },
    {
      "year": 2025,
      "revenue_cr": 181963,
      "net_income_cr": 30404,
      "eps": 77.2
    }
  ],
  "top_assumptions": [
    "Revenue growth moderates from the anomalously high 21.1% YoY in FY2022 (boosted by post-COVID demand surge and digital transformation acceleration) toward the 4-year CAGR of approximately 14.6% (FY2018-FY2022: from 70,522 to 1,21,641 crore), blended with management's sustained large-deal momentum (TCV of USD 9.5 billion), digital revenue mix at 57% growing at ~41% CC, and guidance for continued double-digit growth; a blended projection rate of ~18% for FY2023 tapering to ~14% for FY2024 and ~11.5% for FY2025 is applied as demand normalises post the pandemic-era surge.",
    "Net profit margin is assumed to remain broadly stable at ~17.5%-17.7% of consolidated revenues (FY2022 PAT margin was 18.2% on consolidated basis), with modest compression from continued wage inflation, elevated sub-contractor costs and higher travel resumption, partially offset by operating leverage on a larger revenue base and efficiency gains; EPS dilution is kept minimal given ongoing buyback programme reducing weighted average share count."
  ],
  "reasoning": "FY2022 recorded an anomalously high 21.1% revenue growth on the consolidated P&L (revenues jumped from 1,00,472 crore in FY2021 to 1,21,641 crore in FY2022), driven by a post-COVID demand surge and accelerated digital transformation spending that inflated the latest year-on-year rate well above the 4-year CAGR of ~14.6% (FY2018-FY2022). Accordingly, the latest growth rate alone is not a reliable projection basis, and a mean-reversion approach is applied: FY2023 uses ~18% growth (partial persistence of strong deal pipeline and digital revenue momentum), FY2024 uses ~13.5% (normalisation toward CAGR), and FY2025 uses ~11.5% (steady-state). The 5-year historical data from the key trends table (FY2018: 70,522; FY2019: 82,675; FY2020: 90,791; FY2021: 1,00,472; FY2022: 1,21,641 crore) underpins the CAGR anchor. Balance sheet capacity is strong with consolidated cash and investments of 37,419 crore, minimal financial debt, RoE of 29.1%, and ongoing large capex in land and buildings signalling expansion confidence, supporting a growth rate above pure CAGR."
}
Critique: {
  "company": "INFY",
  "base_year": 2022,
  "strategy": "alt_strategy",
  "assumptions": [
    {
      "assumption": "Revenue growth tapers gracefully from ~18% in FY2023 to ~11.5% in FY2025, anchored by a blended CAGR of ~14.6% and sustained large-deal momentum (TCV USD 9.5 billion), implying demand normalisation is orderly and deal conversion remains on track.",
      "failure_scenario": "A sharper-than-expected global IT spending slowdown \u2014 triggered by client budget freezes amid a US/EU recession or aggressive cost-cutting by BFSI and retail verticals (which together represent ~40%+ of Infosys revenues) \u2014 causes deal ramp-ups to be deferred or cancelled, compressing FY2023 revenue growth to the low teens and FY2024-25 growth to high single digits, well below the blended projection.",
      "risk": "High"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "Net profit margins remain broadly stable at 17.5%-17.7%, with wage inflation and sub-contractor cost pressures only partially eroding FY2022's 18.2% PAT margin, offset by operating leverage and efficiency gains.",
      "failure_scenario": "Attrition remains structurally elevated (Infosys reported ~27% annualised attrition in FY2022), forcing sustained off-cycle wage hikes and aggressive lateral hiring at premium costs; simultaneously, a stronger INR against the USD compresses export realisation, pushing PAT margins below 16% and rendering the net income figures materially overstated.",
      "risk": "High"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "EPS dilution remains minimal because the ongoing buyback programme sufficiently offsets any share count increases from ESOP issuances, preserving the per-share earnings trajectory implied by the forecast.",
      "failure_scenario": "Regulatory constraints or capital allocation reprioritisation (e.g., a large acquisition or elevated capex cycle) curtail or suspend the buyback programme, while ESOP-related dilution continues unabated; the weighted average share count rises meaningfully, causing reported EPS to undershoot forecast EPS figures by 3%-5% even if net income targets are met.",
      "risk": "Medium"
    }
  ],
  "confidence_score": 52,
  "overall_critique": "The forecast is methodologically sound in applying mean-reversion to an anomalous base year, but it materially underweights the macro demand risk heading into FY2023-25, particularly the sensitivity of Infosys's discretionary IT revenue to a synchronised global slowdown that was already becoming visible at the time of this forecast's preparation. Margin stability assumptions are optimistic given the structural wage and attrition pressures acknowledged but not sufficiently stress-tested, making the net income and EPS projections vulnerable to a compounding downside if both revenue growth and margins disappoint simultaneously."
}

Step 1: For each "High" risk assumption in the critique, adjust the corresponding forecast values by a conservative factor (reduce growth by 20-30% for that driver).
Step 2: For "Medium" risk assumptions, reduce growth by 10% for that driver.
Step 3: Leave "Low" risk assumptions unchanged.
Step 4: Calculate a confidence range: ± percentage based on the number of High (±15%) and Medium (±8%) risks.

RESPONSE FORMAT: Begin your response DIRECTLY with the opening brace { — no reasoning or text before the JSON.

Return ONLY valid JSON:
{
  "strategy": "<strategy>",
  "base_year": <YYYY>,
  "revised_forecast": [
    {"year": <YYYY>, "revenue_cr": <number>, "net_income_cr": <number>, "eps": <number>,
     "confidence_range_pct": <number>},
    ...
  ],
  "revisions_made": ["<description of each change>"],
  "final_confidence_score": <0-100>
}
